---
// About (English only): the project, the source work, the digital edition, the creator, credits/licence.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/pages/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import SectionHead from '../../components/ui/SectionHead.astro';
import { meta } from '../../lib/data';
import { fmtInt } from '../../components/ui/format';
import { readRepoFile, renderMarkdown, splitTitle } from '../../components/pages/content';
import { t } from '../../i18n/ui';
import * as u from '../../lib/urls';

const m = meta();
const [authorTitle, authorBody] = splitTitle(readRepoFile('AUTHOR.md'));
const authorHtml = renderMarkdown(authorBody);
---
<Base lang="en" title="About — Elucidário Madeirense" description="About the Elucidário Madeirense digital edition: the source work, the creator, and this project." multilingual={false} kind="about">
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: t('en', 'back_home'), href: u.home('pt') }, { label: t('en', 'about') }]} />
  <SectionHead size="xl" title={t('en', 'about')} role="ink" rule="below" />

  <article class="em-about">
    <section aria-labelledby="h-project">
      <SectionHead size="l" title={t('en', 'about_project')} id="h-project" role="person" n="1" />
      <p>The <strong>Elucidário Madeirense</strong> digital edition makes the 1921/1940 encyclopaedia of Madeira searchable,
        cross-linked and readable in four languages. It carries forward {fmtInt(m.counts.articles, 'en')} articles,
        {fmtInt(m.counts.persons, 'en')} people, {fmtInt(m.counts.places, 'en')} places and {fmtInt(m.counts.events, 'en')} dated
        events into a single structured dataset, with every person, place and date resolved into its own page and linked back to
        the articles that mention it.</p>
      <p>The edition is published in Portuguese (the original language), English, Ukrainian and Hungarian. Where a translation
        is not yet available for a given piece of text, the page shows the English metadata or the Portuguese original, marked
        accordingly, rather than leaving a gap.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="h-source">
      <SectionHead size="l" title={t('en', 'about_source_work')} id="h-source" role="date" n="2" />
      <p><em>Elucidário Madeirense</em> was first published in two volumes in 1921, to mark the fifth centenary of the discovery
        of Madeira, under the direction of <strong>Fernando Augusto da Silva</strong> and <strong>Carlos Azevedo de Meneses</strong>,
        with contributions from Adolfo César de Noronha and Alberto Artur Sarmento. A revised and expanded edition in three
        volumes followed in 1940–46, with further collaboration from Alberto Artur Sarmento. The work set out to be, in the
        words of its own prospectus, "historical in character but popular in style... easy and quick to consult", covering the
        archipelago's history, biography, natural history, customs, and economic and literary life in several thousand short
        entries arranged alphabetically.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="h-edition">
      <SectionHead size="l" title={t('en', 'about_digital_edition')} id="h-edition" role="place" n="3" />
      <p>The text was transcribed and OCR-corrected from the original volumes, segmented into individual articles, and
        structured into headwords, chapters, body text, tables, verse and cross-references. People, places and chronology
        events mentioned anywhere in the text were identified, de-duplicated and linked, and places were additionally resolved
        to coordinates and administrative units (parish, municipality, island, or country and continent for places abroad).</p>
      <p>Translation into English, Ukrainian and Hungarian followed a documented house style (see
        <a href={u.about('technical')}>Technical notes</a>) covering names, religious titles, historical figures, specialist
        terminology and numbers, with per-language style guides and a glossary ("termbase") of about 1,200 Madeira- and
        Portugal-specific terms. Decisions on contested renderings were recorded and are published alongside the guides.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="h-creator">
      <SectionHead size="l" title={t('en', 'about_creator')} id="h-creator" role="person" n="4" />
      {authorTitle && <p class="em-author-name">{authorTitle}</p>}
      <div class="em-prose" set:html={authorHtml} />
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="h-credits">
      <SectionHead size="l" title={t('en', 'about_credits')} id="h-credits" role="ink" n="5" />
      <p>This digital edition was produced with the assistance of large-language-model translation and editorial tooling,
        under human review, as documented in <a href={u.about('technical')}>Technical notes</a>.</p>
      <p class="em-flag"><strong>Licence note (placeholder — to be confirmed by the project owner).</strong> The original 1921
        text is in the public domain in Portugal (author died 1949, more than 70 years ago). The 1940/46 revision's additional
        authorship, and the modern transcription, translations and dataset, are the property of this project; a specific
        open licence for the digital edition (for example CC BY-SA for text and CC0 for the structured data) has not yet been
        selected. Treat all text here as "all rights reserved, pending" until this note is replaced.</p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>
<style>
  .em-about { max-width: var(--measure); margin: 0 auto; padding: var(--s-5) var(--gutter) var(--s-9); }
  .em-about section { margin-bottom: var(--s-7); }
  .em-about p { margin: 0 0 1em; }
  .em-author-name { font-family: var(--font-didone); font-style: italic; font-size: var(--fs-lede); color: var(--person-ink); }
  .em-flag { border-left: 3px solid var(--date); padding-left: var(--s-4); font-size: var(--fs-small); opacity: .9; }
  .em-prose :global(img) { max-width: 12rem; float: right; margin: 0 0 1em 1.5em; }
  .em-prose :global(h2) { font-size: var(--fs-h4); margin: 1.2em 0 .4em; }
  .em-prose :global(ul) { padding-left: 1.3em; }
</style>
